I once saw a candidate lose a DevOps job in 60 seconds.

The interviewer asked:

“A service is slow in production. What will you check?”

The candidate answered:

“CPU usage… memory… logs…”

Not wrong.

But not enough.

Because performance issues are rarely about just one metric.

Here’s how experienced engineers approach it 

---

When an application becomes slow:

✔ Check latency & response time trends
✔ Look for recent deployments or config changes
✔ Inspect database query performance
✔ Verify external API response times
✔ Check thread pools / connection limits
✔ Look for traffic spikes or load imbalance

Sometimes the server is healthy…

…but the database is locked.
…or an external API is timing out.
…or a new release introduced slow queries.

---

Interview insight

If you answer with only CPU & memory,
it shows basic knowledge.

If you consider dependencies, latency, and recent changes,
it shows real production thinking.

That’s what interviewers want to see.

---

Performance issues aren’t always resource problems.

They’re system problems.
